From: EasyMesh labs
Subject: [PATCH] wmediumd: a frame waits at most the queue bound for the air

A frame is scheduled for the time the air is free for it, however far away. Offered
faster than the air carries, the queue grows without bound and never drains: on rdk-1009
(the K8, 9 October) a ping load from 100 clients left 172,244 frames queued, each waiting
28 minutes, the medium serving about 147 frames a second against about 5,000 offered;
every beacon and backhaul frame came half an hour late and every Wi-Fi backhaul stayed
down for good. After a lab's bring-up the delay reached 4 s (rdk-1004, the same day) and
pings were lost on clients of every access point.

A frame that would wait longer than the queue bound (-B, microseconds; default 1 s; 0:
no bound) is dropped as a radio's full transmit queue drops it: its status goes back
unacknowledged, it is freed, and a radio queue goes on with its next frame (in a loop,
not recursively). The drops are counted in the telemetry summary (drops_queue_bound,
appended: the summary is now 256 bytes) and logged at most once a second. The self-test
offers 100 frames to a 50 ms bound: the kept ones start within it, the rest are dropped,
a frame on the free air afterwards goes at once, and with no bound every frame waits.

